  "result": "Riley, your s1 answers came out **2 correct, 2 partial and 2 wrong**.\n\n| Item | Verdict | Main point |\n|---|---|---|\n| q1 | Correct | The loop is what makes an agent. |\n| q2 | Wrong | The model writes a tool request; your program runs the tool. |\n| q3 | Correct | A step limit stops a stuck agent. |\n| q4 | Wrong | There are 4 model calls: three tool requests and one final answer. |\n| q5 | Partial | You said where the result goes, but not why. |\n| q6 | Partial | Tool names and descriptions are right, but the model doesn't remember earlier sessions on its own. |\n\n**Summary:** you know that results come back to the model in a loop, and that a step limit protects you when it gets stuck. The main thing to work on is **who does what**. The model only writes text, such as a tool request or an answer, and your program runs the tools. Mixing these up affected both q2 and q4. Also, memory between sessions isn't automatic (q6).\n\n**Next step:** on paper, trace \"Book me the 3D printer on Friday if I'm trained on it\". Label every step \"model\" or \"program\", then count the model calls.\n\nFeedback for each answer is in `grades/0001-s1.json`. I only graded s1, so your later attempt, s2, is still ungraded.",
